module lib/hf/orthogonalize
  Basis functions on neighboring atoms overlap, so the SCF's eigenproblem
  `F C = S C e` carries the overlap matrix S. An orthogonalizer is a matrix X
  with `X^T S X = I`; with it the SCF diagonalizes the ordinary symmetric
  matrix `X^T F X` and maps the eigenvectors back with X. Many X satisfy
  that, and the energy and density do not depend on which one is used, so
  the module has four, picked by `opts[OPT_ORTH]`: canonical (production),
  Lowdin's symmetric one, Cholesky, and Gram-Schmidt. The last two build the
  same triangular X by different operations, so they check each other.

  Canonical orthogonalization may drop combinations of basis functions that
  are nearly linearly dependent. Dropped columns are zero and come last, so
  `orth_rank(X)` gives m, the size of the orthonormal basis the SCF works in.

orth_threshold  @566c5632
  why    The option column is zero when a caller built the array by hand;
         zero there means the default, not "drop nothing".
  opts   the options column array

orth_canon  @c2365ee5
  why    A near-zero eigenvalue of S means some combination of basis functions
         is almost the zero function; its column would be divided by the
         square root of that eigenvalue, amplifying rounding, and it
         describes nothing the other functions cannot. Dropping it is what
         PySCF does, with the same default threshold
         [test "orth_canon drops exactly one vector on the diffuse H4 chain"].
  S      the overlap matrix of the basis functions, `[n, n]`
  B      not read: the member signature's second matrix
  opts   the options column array; its `OPT_LINDEP` column is the threshold
  n      the number of basis functions
  R      the eigenvalues of S (row `0`) and its eigenvectors (the rows below), from `eig_ql`
  thr    the threshold: eigenvalues at or below it are dropped
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, n]`, kept columns first, dropped ones zero
  c      how many columns have been kept
  k      the eigenvalue being considered

orth_polish  @1a0684e2
  why    It corrects the kept columns so `X^T S X = I` holds to rounding even
         when an eigenvalue of S is small, without changing the space the
         columns span, so the SCF's answer is unchanged.
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  X      the orthogonalizer, corrected in place over its first m columns
  m      how many columns are kept
  E      the defect `X^T S X - I`, `[m, m]`, formed with compensated sums

orth_prod_err  @bed9cf88
  why    The compensated sums in `orth_gram_err` need the exact rounding error
         of each product; this computes it with ordinary arithmetic, so the
         interpreter and the compiled program get the same bits.
  a      the first factor
  b      the second factor
  p      the rounded product of a and b
  ca     a times the splitting constant, used to split a
  ah     the high half of a's bits
  al     the low half: `a = ah + al` exactly
  cb     b times the splitting constant
  bh     the high half of b's bits
  bl     the low half of b's bits

orth_gram_err  @8c59533d
  why    The defect of X is far smaller than the rounding of an ordinary sum
         of products of X's large entries, so it can only be measured, and
         corrected, with sums that keep their own rounding error.
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]` used
  m      how many columns
  n      the number of basis functions
  E      the defect `X^T S X - I`, `[m, m]`
  th     `S x_b`, the high part, `[n]`
  tl     `S x_b`, the part th lost to rounding
  s      a running sum
  c      the running sum's accumulated rounding error
  p      one rounded product
  e      its rounding error
  t      the rounded sum of s and p
  z      the part of p that went into t, for the two-sum error
  h      one entry of `S x_b`, rounded

  = th     `S x_b` as a high part and a correction, so the second product loses nothing
  = c#4    Knuth's two-sum error of the addition, plus the product's error, plus the correction term's product
  = s#5    subtract the identity where it is exact

orth_symm  @04c1cf7a
  why    Lowdin's `S^(-1/2)` is the orthonormal basis closest to the original
         functions, and it drops nothing, so it is a second eigen-based member
         to compare with the canonical one.
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  B      not read
  opts   the options column array
  n      the number of basis functions
  R      the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of S
  X      `U s^(-1/2) U^T`, `[n, n]`
  s      one entry being summed
  i      the row of X
  j      the column of X
  k      the eigenvalue whose term is being added

orth_cholesky  @56bfbf12
  why    The Cholesky member needs the factor, and it is short enough to write
         here rather than depend on a builtin.
  S      the overlap matrix, symmetric positive definite, `[n, n]`
  n      its size
  L      the lower triangular factor, `S = L L^T`
  s      the diagonal entry being reduced
  t      an entry below the diagonal being reduced

orth_chol  @255fde14
  why    An orthogonalizer that needs no eigenproblem, so it checks the others
         independently of the eigensolver.
  S      the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  B      not read
  opts   not read
  n      the number of basis functions
  L      the Cholesky factor of S
  X      `L^(-T)`, upper triangular, `[n, n]`
  c      the column of X being solved for
  i      the row being solved, from the bottom up
  t      the right-hand side minus the rows already solved
  k      a row already solved

orth_sdot  @cb118788
  why    Gram-Schmidt needs the inner product in the S metric, `u^T S v`.
  S      the overlap matrix
  U      the matrix holding u as a column
  a      u's column
  V      the matrix holding v as a column
  b      v's column

orth_gs  @88f0ad17
  why    In exact arithmetic Gram-Schmidt gives the same triangular X as
         Cholesky, by different operations, so the two can be compared entry
         by entry [test "Cholesky and Gram-Schmidt build the same triangular X by different operations"].
         Each vector is orthogonalized twice, which is enough to remove what
         the first pass left behind.
  S      the overlap matrix
  B      not read
  opts   not read
  n      the number of basis functions
  X      the orthonormalized unit vectors, one per column, `[n, n]`
  c      the vector being orthonormalized
  pass   which of the two passes
  p      an earlier column c is made orthogonal to

orth_methods  @91bdde7b
  why    The SCF picks its orthogonalizer by number, and the tests loop over
         every member.

orth_rank  @a3debc69
  why    Callers need m, the size of the orthonormal basis, and the dropped
         columns are the zero ones.
  X      the orthogonalizer, `[n, n]`
  m      how many columns are nonzero
  c      the column being examined

orth_of  @31d5b5e5
  why    The SCF and the guesses call one def whatever orthogonalizer the
         options pick. It calls each member by name, so the default options
         reach canonical orthogonalization without a registry.
  S      the overlap matrix
  opts   the options column array; `opts[OPT_ORTH]` picks the member
  k      the member the options name: `ORTH_CANON`, `ORTH_SYMM`, `ORTH_CHOL` or `ORTH_GS`

orth_defect  @efa4721e
  why    The tests hold every member to `X^T S X = I`; this is the size of the
         miss, measured accurately enough to test to rounding.
  S      the overlap matrix
  X      the orthogonalizer
  m      how many columns to test

orth_inverse_defect  @eac867c9
  why    With full rank, `X X^T = S^(-1)` for every orthogonalizer, which
         compares members without depending on which X each picked
         [test "with full rank every member's X X^T is S^-1"].
  S      the overlap matrix
  X      the orthogonalizer
  E      `X X^T S - I`
  i      a diagonal position

orth_check_all  @3c6fa02e
  why    One call runs every member on one overlap matrix for the tests.
  S      the overlap matrix
  mwant  how many columns every member should keep
  ms     the registry
  o      the default options
  worst  the worst defect so far
  nbad   how many members kept a different number of columns
  X      one member's orthogonalizer
  m      how many columns it kept
  k      the member
